    Z1 exact ceiling moves with n:         True   [16, 64, 256]
    Z2 and does so ~linearly (1.4-3x):    False
    Z3 sparse inflation GROWS with n:     False   sparse/exact = 2.0x 1.0x 1.0x
    Z4 ff has no ceiling, either engine:   True   exact [256, 256, 256]  sparse [256, 256, 256]

    against critical-load alpha* (M_max ~ 1.15 n/k):
       predicted  n=1000 M=23  n=2000 M=46  n=4000 M=92
       exact      n=1000 M=16  n=2000 M=64  n=4000 M=256
       sparse     n=1000 M=32  n=2000 M=64  n=4000 M=256

    The ceiling scales with n on exact drive, and the sampler's
    inflation does NOT grow with n -- so the scaling is real and
    the instrument is not what produced it. brain.py's inference
    was right for a reason it had not established.
